  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "De quién es",
    "@en": "Whose is it?"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "En inglés, **my, your, his, her, our, their** conservan la misma forma ante un nombre singular o plural: **my brother / my brothers**. El posesivo cambia según quién posee. En español: **my** (mi/mis), **your** (tu/tus), **his** (su/sus, de él), **her** (su/sus, de ella), **our** (nuestro/a/os/as), **their** (su/sus, de ellos/ellas).",
     "@en": "In English, **my, your, his, her, our, their** retain the same form before a singular or plural name: **my brother / my brothers**. The possessive changes according to who owns. In Spanish: **my** (mi / mis), **your** (tu / tus), **his** (su / sus, from him), **her** (su / sus, from her), **our** (ours / os / as), **their** (su / sus, from them / them)."
    },
    {
     "@es": "En español «su» vale para varias personas; en inglés hay que elegir: **his sister** (la hermana de él), **her sister** (la hermana de ella).",
     "@en": "In Spanish «its» is for several people; in English you have to choose: **his sister** (his sister), **her sister** (her sister)."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Con un nombre singular, se añade **'s** para indicar posesión: **Jack's wife** (la esposa de Jack), **my mother's name** (el nombre de mi madre).",
     "@en": "With a singular name, **'s** is added to indicate possession: **Jack's wife** (Jack's wife), **my mother's name** (my mother's name)."
    }
   ]
  },
